1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
In medical device software, changing one firmware
constant doesn't just change code.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
A bolus clamp limit from 5.0 to 5.5 mL/hr
invalidates a clinical hazard assessment,

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
a safety requirement, a verification test,
and an FDA electronic signature.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:25,000
IEC 62304 and ISO 14971 demand you prove
that chain is intact.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
MedTrace does it live, inside Jira and Confluence —
with zero data leaving Atlassian.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
One click seeds a Smart Infusion Pump project.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Note the badge — every record is clearly labeled
as modeled demo data.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
We never fake tenant evidence —
that discipline is the product.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:55,000
SRS-104 drifted after its Part 11 signature.
One click runs a breadth-first traversal

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,000
and returns the transitive blast radius:
the signature invalidated,

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
the Class C hazard compromised, Jira task MED-42 locked,
TEST-88 invalidated —

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
plus the exact re-audit checklist.
When two requirements share one hazard,

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
the traversal crosses that shared node —
pinned by a dedicated test in our suite.

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:28,000
This button invokes the exact validator handler
Jira runs natively — with site-admin, attach it

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
to a transition and Jira itself blocks the move
while a Class C hazard is unverified. It fails closed.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Jira Automation bypasses transition validators —
platform issue ECO-624 —

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
so an identical gate runs in CI via an authenticated
webtrigger. A working GitHub Actions workflow ships in the repo.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Verification runs, the electronic signature re-certifies
against the current SHA-256, and the release gate unlocks.

19
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
If the backend were unreachable, you'd get an honest
error banner — never a simulated success.

20
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Everything compiles into a printable 510(k) eSTAR-aligned
traceability dossier — hashes, signatures, test evidence.

21
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:20,000
No external fetch permissions exist in the manifest —
clinical data never leaves the Atlassian tenant.

22
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Mutations arrive through a webtrigger protected by
HMAC-SHA256, a 300-second replay window,

23
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
and persistent nonce consumption in Forge storage.
Replay returns 409 —

24
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
our test suite proves it live:
200, 409, 409, 200.

25
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:52,000
The MedTrace Auditor Rovo agent runs the same
deterministic engine from chat.

26
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:00,000
And through our MCP server, Cursor asks the live
compliance graph before you commit.

27
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:10,000
MedTrace — deterministic regulatory blast radius, a hard
safety gate, and cryptographic evidence. Built on Atlassian Forge.
